[Music]  first days of school a heart I get it  I've been there but my first step middle  school with different I was sitting at  my desk feeling like an outsider at a  new school when I heard someone rush  towards me hey he said we're missing one  for Table Tennis in Chinese it's ping  pong so you in I hooked up being  surprised I I don't know how to play  ping pong it's okay he said we'll teach  you so he dragged me to a game was he  and his friends boys and girls and we  had a really good time now full  disclosure I still suck I have been fun  but I remember the feeling of warmth a  feeling included welcomed into that new  community the game brought me in four  years ago another friend of mine dragged  me to another game with him it was at an  old sports stadium three hours away from  Shanghai and it was boring ring that day  come on I said to him nobody is gonna  travel three hours in this horror line  green to watch people play video games  plus it's gonna be awkward I'm  definitely gonna be the only girl there  well three hours later I was sitting at  the stadium soaking wet and surprised to  find myself surrounded by over 10,000  fans they were young they were excited  the faces were painted in teen colors  and I looked out to the crowd I was  definitely not the only girl there in  fact almost half of the audience was  female when the game started it was like  someone lit a fire in the stadium a  dragon threw across the stream and the  crowd broke into roaring waves of chair  I looked around at the people hiding  around me somewhere older most were  younger  some were men some we're women and I  felt well  the king the video game is bringing  people together and that excited me for  the following three years I co-founded a  eSports company in China aiming to  create experiences just like this to  connect people through eSports and  gaming now fast-forward a couple years  last summer I moved to the US not had  heard about the difference between the  eastern game culture and the Western  game culture namely here it's a little  bit more boy centric so I wanted to  check it out myself I drove up north to  Oakland eSports arena to a game of  fortnight for at night is the massively  popular game here and it's one of the  most gender balance game here  it has over 46 percent of its player  base female I wanted to find a few  female gamers and ask them about about  their experience when I got there well  this is not a 10,000 people arena and  there were no dragons or anything but it  was still very cool it was a cool hip  arena with state-of-art computers when I  walked in the players and the fans were  glued to the screen immersed in their  game and behind them was this big screen  showing what was going on they didn't  notice me when I wanted but I noticed  something I was the only female there  and this is not a standalone incident  I've heard women on podcast in articles  talking about experiences similar to  mine finding themselves to be one of the  very few females at eSports events but  wait a minute if the stats are correct  the percentage of female gamers here in  the u.s. is actually quite comparable to  that in China in both countries almost  half of the gamer base or female and  where are the u.s. female gamers maybe  they just don't like offline events but  then I realize it's not just offline the  female gamers here are not showing up to  offline events and they're invisible  online to  my friend Claire tells me about her  experience  Claire is an excellent overwatch player  she's being playing the game since it  launched and she tells me that when she  plays overwatch she Mews her mic  why do you meet your mic I asked Claire  well she said cuz I don't want him to  find out I'm a woman what would happen  if they find out you're a woman what  would they do to you for one thing she  said they would not invite me to the  next game on the other hand there is  also that awkward private messages hey  how are you what are you doing what are  you wearing  come on I don't want any of that I just  I just want to play my game there is an  underlying assumption here about the  motivation of a female gamer namely if  you can't prove your authenticity if you  can't prove your gaming skills and your  love of gaming by beating every single  person out there that challenges you to  a game then you don't really like gaming  you're just doing it to get guys  attention now this reminds me of an old  joke which says women go to colleges  just to get husbands just to get Mrs  degree now that joke is over a half a  century old but we're here in 2020 and  gaming is new it's no longer that small  niche industry in the 60s and 70s it's  now a giant industry overtaking the  entertainment form the global gaming  industry is a hundred and twenty billion  dollars it's bigger than the global  movie box office and the music industry  combined 2.5 billion people around the  world are gamers so it's now a big  community got a big voice and what kind  of messages are we saying with this  voice  then I realized there's also a second  layer of the issue a less noticeable one  in 2015 there was this incident called  gamergate in which online trolls  attacked female video game producers  with brave threats and death threats  demanding them to get out of the  industry Brianna Wu a female video game  producer right at the center of  gamergate reflected on her experience  recently in her op-ed on new york times  she said I wish I could tell you things  have gone better five years since the  gamergate but they have it  she said we built worlds of fantasy for  men by men for men by men  now the issue we're seeing here in video  games is not fresh we've seen it before  we've seen it in Hollywood we've seen it  in traditional sports we've seen it  right here in Silicon Valley but we have  a unique window of opportunity here the  gaming industry despite its rapid growth  is still growing it's still small it  will grow bigger it's growing to take a  even more dominant part of our social  life and not just our generation but  generations to come  we've got to do something here what can  we do well I see three things to be done  one if you are a gamer well if you're a  female gamer I'll mute your mic speak up  because you deserve to be hurt if you  are male gamer actually mute your mic  try this for a change will you go home  tonight sign on to your favorite game  and create a new ID a female ID with a  female name and see what happens to you  and notice how you feel what you would  like to be changed to if you were run a  game company which I know some of you  may go on to do think about who you are  bringing onto your creative staff think  about who you have on your production  team because the digital world just like  our real world should be created for  everyone by everyone isn't it  three if you are none of the above  well whatever your form of gameplay is  pokiman go Jenga  Texas Hold'em bring someone and invite  someone bring them in when I moved here  last summer I brought my two kids with  me a six year old and a two year old I  remember feeling very nervous taking  them to their first playdate  will he make friends will they be  accepted  coz when they moved here they didn't  speak a lick of English will they be  included or whether it be like their  mother when I was their age well they  just sit silently in the corner when we  got there the party had already started  the kids had laid out toys on the floor  blocks Lego a switch my kids jumped with  joy and immediately they rushed in and  the children started playing the games  brought them in thank you  [Applause]  [Music]

